---
import ArticlePage from "../../components/ArticlePage.astro";
import NuclideRef from "../../components/NuclideRef.astro";
import { mdaExamples, MDA_INPUT } from "../../lib/note-examples";
import { prefillPath } from "../../lib/restore";
import { u } from "../../lib/site";

/** ★ MDA 풀이 — 노리는 검색어: minimum detectable activity · mda calculation · mda calculator(순위 5~30).
 *  ★ 식과 가정은 계산기(`engine/mda.ts` · `Mda.tsx`)와 **같다**: L_C = k√(2B), L_D = k² + 2L_C, 짝 블랭크.
 *    화면에는 방출률 · 시료 질량 칸이 없고 효율 칸 하나(「Counts per emission」)뿐이다 — 글이 그대로 말한다.
 *  ★ 짝 블랭크 밖의 식(잘 아는 블랭크 · 블랭크를 더 오래 잰 경우)은 계산기가 하지 않는다. 글이 그렇다고 적고 손으로 보인다.
 *    출처: Currie 1968 표 I(NIST SP 958 해설에서 읽음) · MARLAP 식 20.8~20.11(2026-11-01 원문 대조). */
const X = mdaExamples();
const I = MDA_INPUT;
const calc = prefillPath("mda", { mode: "scaler", bgCpm: I.bgCpm, timeS: X.t, eff: X.formEffPct, k: "1.645", unit: "Bq" });
const c1 = (x: number) => x.toFixed(1);
const bq3 = (x: number) => x.toPrecision(3);
const [paired, longer, known] = X.blanks;
const WORD = ["Not detected. Below L_C.", "Detected, though below L_D.", "Detected, and above L_D."];
---
<ArticlePage slug="mda-worked-example">
  <p>
    You counted a sample and no peak stood out. What is the smallest activity that count could reliably have
    seen? That figure is the minimum detectable activity, and this note works it through for
    {" "}<NuclideRef k="Cs-137" /> on a gamma spectrometer, line by line, using the equations and the assumptions of the
    {" "}<a href={u("/mda/")} class="underline">MDA calculator</a>: Currie's detection limits with a paired blank.
    Then it reads three real results against the limits, since that is where most confusion starts.
  </p>

  <h2>The measurement</h2>
  <p>The inputs below are illustrative but typical of a shielded detector and a one-hour count.</p>
  <div class="not-prose my-3 pin-first table-fit" tabindex="0">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Input</th><th class="text-right">Value</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Blank count rate in the {X.eKeV.toFixed(0)}&nbsp;keV peak region</td><td class="num text-right">{I.bgCpm}&nbsp;cpm</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Count time t, same for sample and blank</td><td class="num text-right">{X.t}&nbsp;s</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Full-energy peak efficiency ε</td><td class="num text-right">{I.effPct.toFixed(1)}%</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Emission probability Y, IAEA Livechart</td><td class="num text-right">{X.yPct}%</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Sample mass</td><td class="num text-right">{X.massKg}&nbsp;kg</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Coverage k, for 5% false positives and 5% false negatives</td><td class="num text-right">{I.k}</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2>Step 1: the critical level L<sub>C</sub></h2>
  <p>
    The expected blank count is B = {I.bgCpm} × {I.minutes} = {X.B} counts. A net count is the difference of two
    counts that each carry a variance of about B, which is where the 2 under the root comes from:
  </p>
  <p>
    L<sub>C</sub> = k√(2B) = {I.k} × √{2 * X.B} = {I.k} × {X.sqrt2B.toFixed(2)} = <strong>{c1(X.lc)} counts</strong>.
  </p>
  <p>
    This is the decision threshold, applied after the count. A sample with no Cs-137 in it would exceed it in
    only 5% of counts.
  </p>

  <h2>Step 2: the detection limit L<sub>D</sub></h2>
  <p>
    L<sub>D</sub> = k² + 2L<sub>C</sub> = {X.k2.toFixed(3)} + 2 × {c1(X.lc)} = <strong>{c1(X.ld)} counts</strong>.
  </p>
  <p>
    That is the true net signal that would clear L<sub>C</sub> in 95% of counts. It is fixed before the sample is
    measured. At k = {I.k} the expression is the familiar 2.71 + 4.65√B.
  </p>

  <h2>Step 3: from counts to becquerels</h2>
  <p>
    Each becquerel gives ε·Y·t counts: Y photons per decay, ε counts per photon, t seconds. Here that is
    0.030 × {(X.yPct / 100).toFixed(3)} × {X.t} = {c1(X.denom)} counts per becquerel, so
  </p>
  <p>
    MDA = L<sub>D</sub> / (ε·Y·t) = {c1(X.ld)} / {c1(X.denom)} = <strong>{bq3(X.mda)}&nbsp;Bq</strong>.
  </p>
  <p>
    For the {X.massKg}&nbsp;kg sample that is {bq3(X.perKg)}&nbsp;Bq/kg. To reproduce this on the calculator, note
    that its form has one efficiency field, “Counts per emission from the source”, and no field for
    emission probability or sample mass. Enter the product of ε and Y, {X.formEffPct}%, with
    {" "}{I.bgCpm}&nbsp;cpm, {X.t}&nbsp;s and the 95% / 95% option, and
    {" "}<a href={u(calc)} class="underline">it returns the same L<sub>C</sub>, L<sub>D</sub> and {bq3(X.mdaForm)}&nbsp;Bq</a>.
    Divide by the mass yourself.
  </p>

  <h2>Reading a real result</h2>
  <p>
    L<sub>C</sub> is what a result is compared with. The MDA is not a reporting threshold, and treating it as one
    is a frequent error. Three possible net counts from this measurement:
  </p>
  <div class="not-prose my-3 pin-first table-fit" tabindex="0">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Net counts</th><th class="text-right">Activity</th><th>Conclusion</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {X.results.map((r, i) => (
          <tr><td class="num">{r.net}</td><td class="num text-right">{bq3(r.bq)}&nbsp;Bq</td><td>{WORD[i]}</td></tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    The middle row surprises people. A net count of {X.results[1].net} is a detection even though its
    {" "}{bq3(X.results[1].bq)}&nbsp;Bq is below the {bq3(X.mda)}&nbsp;Bq MDA, because the MDA describes what the method
    reliably catches, not what it is allowed to report. In the first row, keep the measured value and its
    uncertainty alongside the words “not detected” instead of writing zero, which is also what ISO 11929
    recommends and which keeps later averages honest.
  </p>

  <h2>Paired blank or well-known blank</h2>
  <p>
    The 2 in √(2B) assumes a paired blank: one blank counted for the same time as the sample, carrying as much
    counting noise as the sample does. Count the blank for longer and its share of the noise shrinks. With
    B still scaled to the sample's count time, MARLAP gives the variance of a net count on an empty sample as
    B(1 + t<sub>S</sub>/t<sub>B</sub>), and then L<sub>C</sub> = k√(B(1 + t<sub>S</sub>/t<sub>B</sub>)) and
    L<sub>D</sub> = k² + 2L<sub>C</sub>. A blank known so well that it adds nothing is Currie's “well-known” case, with
    the expression 2.71 + 3.29√B.
  </p>
  <div class="not-prose my-3 pin-first table-fit" tabindex="0">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Blank</th><th class="text-right">L<sub>C</sub></th><th class="text-right">L<sub>D</sub></th><th class="text-right">MDA</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Paired, t<sub>B</sub> = t<sub>S</sub></td><td class="num text-right">{c1(paired.lc)}</td><td class="num text-right">{c1(paired.ld)}</td><td class="num text-right">{bq3(paired.mda)}&nbsp;Bq</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Longer, t<sub>B</sub> = 4t<sub>S</sub></td><td class="num text-right">{c1(longer.lc)}</td><td class="num text-right">{c1(longer.ld)}</td><td class="num text-right">{bq3(longer.mda)}&nbsp;Bq</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Well-known</td><td class="num text-right">{c1(known.lc)}</td><td class="num text-right">{c1(known.ld)}</td><td class="num text-right">{bq3(known.mda)}&nbsp;Bq</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    The MDA calculator implements only the paired row. If your blank really is longer, its figure errs high and
    you can work the lower one from the expressions above. Be careful about claiming the well-known row. A
    background that drifts, or a blank from a different day, adds variation these Poisson expressions do not
    contain, and the lower figure is then not earned.
  </p>

  <h2>Where MDA figures go wrong</h2>
  <ul class="my-3 list-disc space-y-2 pl-5">
    <li><strong>Quoting L<sub>C</sub> as the limit.</strong> That gives {bq3(X.lcAsMda)}&nbsp;Bq here, too low by a factor of {X.lcRatio.toFixed(1)}.</li>
    <li><strong>Leaving out the emission probability.</strong> Y omitted, the answer reads {bq3(X.noYield)}&nbsp;Bq, {X.noYieldPct.toFixed(0)}% low.</li>
    <li><strong>Using the whole spectrum as background.</strong> Only counts in the peak region compete with the peak. At 200&nbsp;cpm of total background the figure becomes {bq3(X.wholeSpectrum)}&nbsp;Bq, nearly ten times too high.</li>
    <li><strong>Expecting time to do what it cannot.</strong> Four hours instead of one gives {bq3(X.four)}&nbsp;Bq, better by {X.fourRatio.toFixed(2)} times rather than four.</li>
    <li><strong>Forgetting decay.</strong> The equations assume the activity holds steady during the count, and they refer to the time of counting. For <NuclideRef k="I-131" /> counted {X.i131Days} days after sampling, the MDA at the sampling date is {X.i131Factor.toFixed(2)} times larger. A count lasting a full half-life sees on average {X.oneHalfAvgPct.toFixed(1)}% of the starting activity. The calculator applies neither correction; the <a href={u("/decay/")} class="underline">decay calculator</a> gives the factor.</li>
    <li><strong>Very low blanks.</strong> MARLAP notes that the critical-level expression works well from about 100 blank counts, and can produce too many false positives below that. At B = 0 it gives L<sub>C</sub> = 0, so a single count would be called a detection.</li>
    <li><strong>Not saying which convention was used.</strong> MARSSIM writes L<sub>D</sub> with 3 in place of k², which here gives {c1(X.marssimLd)} counts, {X.marssimPct.toFixed(2)}% higher; the <a href={u("/validation/mda/")} class="underline">validation report</a> measures that difference. The form's 99% option, k = 2.326, gives {bq3(X.k99.mda)}&nbsp;Bq. ISO 11929 also carries the uncertainty of the efficiency into its limits, which this calculator does not. State the method beside the number.</li>
  </ul>
</ArticlePage>
